# A match-time trap is recorded once per derivation of its pair
# ([LERR.1.4.12]). `105_released_trap` with the NOT node and the trapping join
# on one schema, so a single `B` is both the blocker `b` and the candidate
# `c`, and both nodes sit on one alpha memory.
#
#   TRANSIENT (`113_transient_trap`). With `a1` live, assert `b1` with
#   `b1.k == a1.k` and `y = 0`. The trapping join may evaluate `((a1), b1)`
#   and trap, but the NOT node on the same fact blocks `(a1)`, so the pair is
#   not live when the assert's propagation completes: nothing is recorded,
#   whatever order the two nodes are right-activated in (formal witness
#   `lerr_1_4_12__transient_pair_records_nothing`).
#
#   RE-DERIVED (`114_rederived_trap`). `c1` (another key, `y = 0`) traps
#   against `(a1)`; `b1` (a1's key, `y = 1`) blocks `(a1)` and ends the pair;
#   retracting `b1` releases `(a1)` and derives `((a1), c1)` again, and it
#   records again. Two traps.
#
# `x` and `y` are Int: only an all-Int division traps [LEXP.7.15.1].
use default

schema A scope logical:
    k: String key

schema B scope logical:
    k: String key
    x: Int mutable
    y: Int mutable

rule r:
    A a
    no B b where b.k == a.k
    B c where c.x / c.y > 1.0
->
    log info "r fired"
